Byte slices are just like int slices or string slices
However notice that strings are not char slices
In fact, char doesn't even exist in Go
rather strings are byte slices which are immutable 

====
In Go, instead of char, we have rune , this is just an alias for int32
=====

package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    var ch rune = 'A'  // Single quotes for a rune
    fmt.Println(ch)     // Outputs: 65 (ASCII code of 'A')
    fmt.Printf("%c\n", ch) // Outputs: A
}


====

[]int => int slices
[]string => string slices 
[]char => char slices 

Similarly []byte is a byte slice 
It is generally used to hold raw binary data 
A byte in Go is an alias for uint8, which represents an 8-bit unsigned integer.

So []byte is equivalent to []uint8

var b []byte = []byte{'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'}
var u []uint8 = []uint8{72, 101, 108, 108, 111}  // Same as the byte slice, but in numeric form

fmt.Println(b)  // Output: [72 101 108 108 111]
fmt.Println(u)  // Output: [72 101 108 108 111]

================

Hence byte arrays are generally used for string manipulation

Strings are immutable in Go, meaning that when you create a new string by
modifying an existing one, it creates a new copy of the string. 
This can be inefficient if you're performing many modifications or 
concatenations, because each modification creates a new string object.

=======
var str string
str = "Hello"
str = str + " World"  // Creates a new string each time, which can be inefficient
=======
Strings in Go are immutable, so every time you modify a string (like appending or changing characters), Go needs to allocate new memory to hold the modified string. This process can lead to inefficiencies in situations where you need to repeatedly modify the string.
By using []byte, you can work with a mutable slice, allowing you to modify the content in-place and avoid excessive reallocation.

[]byte Allows Efficient In-Place Modifications

str := []byte("Hello")
str[0] = 'h'  // Modify 'H' to 'h'
fmt.Println(string(str))  // Output: hello
=========

Other than string manipulation, byte slices are also used in 
File operations, network communications and binary data manipulation
===

Converting from bytes slices to strings and vice versa

s := "Hello"
byteSlice := []byte(s)  // Convert string to []byte
fmt.Println(byteSlice)  // Output: [72 101 108 108 111]

newStr := string(byteSlice)  // Convert back to string
fmt.Println(newStr)  // Output: "Hello"
======

So LONG STORY short, bytes slices are more efficient and 
also more flexible 